0 Hymenophore lamellate, forming wrinkled, bright yellow 'gills' 01111330 Xerocomus pelletieri0
0 Hymenophore typical boletoid tubes and pores 011213402
0 Growing attached to the Earthball Scleroderma citrinum 02231450 Pseudoboletus parasiticus (see p. 10)0
0 Growing on soil in leaf litter022414603
0 All parts of the fruitbody, externally and internally instantly deep cobalt blue-black when touched or cut; cap deep reddish-brown, purplish-bay, stem yellow, smooth, flushed red or purple-brown 03451570 Boletus pulverulentus0
0 Fruitbody may blue when touched or damaged but never entirely and instantly blue-black throughout034615804
0 Entire fungus pale apricot-buff, tawny yellowish, context cream and unchanging, stipe base swollen-radicant, spores broadly ellipsoid, almost hyaline under microscope, associated with Quercus in southern areas 04671690 Boletus moravicus0
0 Colours various but spores subfusifoid, pale to dark brown under microscope0468161005
0 Cap usually plush-velvet (although becoming polished when dry), some shade of rich cinnamon-brown, yellow, olive-brown to rich greenish or bright rust-brown; cap cuticle hyphae long, flexuose and smooth (Fig. 62) 058917110 *** Xerocomus s.str. ***6
0 Cap if plush-velvet then of a different hue (black, deep brown, red, orange etc) but usually smooth and often tesselated with age; cap cuticle hyphae often short, with irregular transverse encrustations (Fig. 62) 0581017120 *** Xerocomellus ***9
0 Associated with Populus, cap rich cinnamon-brown to rust, stipe with radicant base and coarse network at apex, flesh greyish-pink to reddish in lower stipe 0691118130 Xerocomus silwoodensis0
0 Usually associated with Quercus, Betula, conifers etc, stipe mayor may not be radicant and mayor may not have a network 06912181407
0 Stipe tapered at base with bright golden-yellow mycelium, stipe surface with reddish floccules at apex which may coalesce to form a crude network, flesh bright golden yellow in stipe base, associated with Quercus 07121319150 Xerocomus chrysonemus0
0 Stipe usually clavate at base but may also taper, mycelium cream to pale yellowish, never golden, flesh in stipe base not golden yellow, with both broadleaf and coniferous trees 071214191608
0 Context when cut white to ivory, without faint yellow hue, often flushing pinkish, stipe with tendency to narrow at the apex, with or without coarse network, cap colour very variable, yellowish, cinnamon, green, reddish brown 08141520170 Xerocomus ferrugineus0
0 Context when cut with distinct lemon-yellow flush especially in cap, flushing reddish in stipe (occasionally bluish in cap), apex of stipe often broader, stipe surface with or without a brief punctate network 08141620180 Xerocomus subtomentosus0
0 Cap some shade of uniformly bright red, blood-red, pink or pink-orange0910172119010
0 Cap blackish, grey-brown, sepia, reddish-brown, buff-brown, blonde, olive-brown or flushed pinkish-red with age on outer cap0910182120016
0 Flesh at stipe base and cap surface instantly deep blue-green with FeS04; cap scarlet to apricot-red, often tesselated; flesh in stipe base apricot-yellow; cap cuticle hyphae rather long and slender, barely encrusted, often (not always!) with congophilous plaques when stained with congo red 010171922210 Xerocomellus armeniacus0
0 With only weak or negative FeS04 reaction, cap cuticle hyphae often short and broad, clearly encrusted, and without congophilous plaques01017202222011
0 Spores with high proportion distinctly truncate at apex01120212323012
0 Spores with rounded-pointed apex01120222324013
0 Cap bright red, cracking overall, stipe yellowish with red floccules, context and pores only faintly blueing, spores with smooth surface even under high magnification (x 1500), associated with Quercus in Bohemia (not yet British) 012212324250 Xerocomellus marekii*0
0 Cap bright red, cracking mostly in outer regions, stipe also bright red overall, fading to brown, context and pores strongly blueing, spores with faintly longitudinally striate surface under high magnification, associated with Betula and Alnus in wet areas (not yet British) 012212424260 Xerocomellus fennicus*0
0 Growing in damp, often riverine habitats where the soil rarely dries out, usuaUy with Salix or Alnus but also known with Populus, Tilia and Quercus. Entire fruitbody is bright carmine red, cap cuticle rapidly cracks, context stains deep blue throughout, spores are longitudinally striate under high magnification 013222525270 Xerocomellus ripariellus0
0 Growing in drier woodland or parkland habitat or other characters different 01322262528014
0 Typically entire fruitbody is bright scarlet to vermillion, cap plush, velvety when young, slowly cracking, context pale yellow staining blue above the tubes and down into the stipe. Flesh in stipe base dotted with flame orange dots. Spores are smooth. In grass under Quercus 014262726290 Xerocomellus rubellus0
0 Cap usually a more purplish-red, blood red, often with brown tones, stem colour usually mixed yellow and red; context without flame orange dots; spores may be striated01426282630015
0 Context slowly staining deep blue throughout (5-20 mins), stipe typically with bright, bicoloured appearance, yellow above, red punctate-striate below, cap brownish to flushed red overall, spores slender (4.5-5.7 µm wide) finely striated, common under Quercus in the south 015282927310 Xerocomellus cisalpinus0
0 Context hardly blueing, yellow with slight reddening under cuticle, deep rhubarb red in stipe base; stipe yellow above, deep purplish-red below, cap carmine-red, spores are broad (5.5-7 µm wide), smooth, Mediterranean species 015283027320 Xerocomellus dryophilus* sensu European authors0
0 Mature spores with a high proportion truncated (Fig. 2), entire fruitbody rather ugly sepia brown, dingy, stipe mostly sepia with a reddish zone at apex, yellow above the zone 016183128330 Xerocomellus porosporus0
0 Spores with rounded or pointed ends, colours differ01618322834017
0 Context at base of stipe with numerous tiny, flame-orange spots, these may coalesce to form an orange patch; cap ochre-brown flushing reddish with age, stem typically yellowish flushed pink-red. Blueing is usually restricted to cap context and upper stipe 017323329350 Xerocomellus engelii340
0///= Xerocomus quercinus Engel///= Xerocomus communis (Bull.) Bon017323429360N0
0 Context at stipe base without numerous flame orange spots (use hand lens) although X. bubalinus may (rarely) have a very few scattered red spots 01732352937018
0 Context when cut either turning deep blue throughout stipe or at least at base of stipe (may take 5-20 mins), spores finely striated (visible only with a high quality x l000 oil objective)01835363038019
0 Context only blueing slightly in cap especially above tubes, rarely below upper stipe, spores smooth 01835373039020
0 Stipe context typically bright yellow when young, blueing slightly in lower half; cap a beautiful plum-brown when young with reddish margin, often wrinkled-bumpy, tesselated when old, mycelium prominent and yellow, spores finely striated 019363831400 Xerocomellus pruinatus0
0 Stipe context typically dull cream to pale yellowish, intensely blue throughout (5-20 mins), externally the stipe is usually bicoloured yellow above and reddish below; cap is ochre-brown, blackish-brown to flushed red, spores finely striated 019363931410 Xerocomellus cisalpinus0
0 Stipe typically deep red punctate-striate throughout its length, cap dark brown, blackish when young, cracking to reveal pale or reddish flesh; context in stipe usually deep red in outer cortex, slightly blue in above tubes, under conifers or Fagus 020374032420 Xerocomellus chrysenteron0
0 Stipe typically yellow-brown with pinkish-red flush or striae, to entirely red flushed; cap starts deep plumbrown to reddish then soon blonde-ochre, ochre brown and Oushing pinkish or reddish-brown; context pink in cap, with slight to deep blueing above the tubes and upper stipe; cuticular hyphae often bifurcating at tips. British collections so far under Populus and Tilia, possibly Quercus also 020374132430 Xerocomellus bubalinus0
